An impressive centerpiece dessert built inside a tall springform pan. The all-butter crust rises three inches high to hold a bounty of cold-weather produce: tender apples, fragrant pears, plump simmered figs, and jewel-like tart cranberries. A generous blanket of buttery cinnamon-walnut crumb bakes into a crisp, crunchy streusel crown.

*Adapted from [Epicurious](http://www.epicurious.com/recipes/food/views/deep-dish-winter-fruit-pie-with-walnut-crumb-354977).*

## Ingredients

### Flaky All-Butter Pastry
- 1 ¾ cups (8 ¾ oz / 250g) all-purpose flour
- 1 tablespoon granulated sugar
- ½ teaspoon fine sea salt
- 12 tablespoons (1 ½ sticks / 6 oz) cold unsalted butter, cut into ½-inch cubes
- 3 tablespoons ice-cold water
- 1 teaspoon freshly squeezed lemon juice

### Walnut Crumb Topping
- ¾ cup (3 ¾ oz / 105g) all-purpose flour
- ¾ cup packed (5 ¾ oz / 160g) dark brown sugar
- ¾ cup (3 oz / 85g) raw walnuts, coarsely chopped
- 1 ½ teaspoons ground cinnamon
- ¼ teaspoon fine sea salt
- 6 tablespoons (3 oz) unsalted butter, melted

### Winter Fruit Filling
- 1 cup (5 ½ oz / 155g) dried Mission or Turkish figs, stems trimmed off
- 4 small apples (approx. 12 oz prepped), peeled, cored, and sliced ½ inch thick
- 4 firm-ripe pears (such as Bosc or Anjou, approx. 1 ¼ lbs prepped), peeled, cored, and sliced ½ inch thick
- 1 cup (4 oz) fresh or frozen cranberries
- ½ cup (3 ½ oz / 100g) granulated sugar
- 2 tablespoons cornstarch

## Instructions

1. **Chill and Cut the Pastry:** In a medium bowl, whisk together the flour, sugar, and salt. Place the bowl in the freezer for 10 minutes to ensure everything is ice-cold. Add the cold cubed butter and toss to coat. Using a pastry blender or two knives, cut the butter into the flour until the pieces resemble pea-sized crumbles with some larger flakes. Combine the ice water and lemon juice, sprinkle over the flour, and toss with a fork just until the dough holds together when squeezed.
2. **Form and Chill the Dough Disk:** Turn the shaggy dough out onto a lightly floured board and press firmly into a 6-inch round disk. Wrap tightly in plastic wrap and chill in the refrigerator for at least 1 hour.
3. **Line the Springform Pan:** Lightly flour your counter and roll the chilled disk into a 14-inch round. Gently ease the rolled sheet into a 9-or-10-inch springform pan (roughly 3 inches deep), pressing it smoothly into the corner creases and up the sides. Trim any excess hanging over the top edge and patch any thin spots. Return the lined pan to the refrigerator to chill for 30 minutes.
4. **Mix the Walnut Streusel:** In a separate bowl, whisk together the flour, brown sugar, chopped walnuts, ground cinnamon, and salt. Pour in the melted butter and use a fork or clean fingertips to toss and pinch the mixture into moist, clumpy crumbs. Refrigerate while preparing the fruit.
5. **Rehydrate and Prep the Fruit:** Preheat your oven to 375°F (190°C) with a baking rack in the lower third position. Bring 1 cup of water to a boil in a small saucepan. Drop in the stemmed figs and boil gently for 5 minutes to soften and plump. Drain and let rest until cool enough to handle, then slice each fig into 4 to 5 pieces. Place sliced figs, peeled apples, pears, and cranberries into a large bowl.
6. **Toss Filling with Thickener:** In a small cup, stir the granulated sugar and cornstarch together to eliminate any starch clumps. Sprinkle this over the fruit and gently toss until every fruit piece is evenly glossed.
7. **Assemble and Bake:** Pile the fruit filling into the chilled springform crust, leveling the top. Scatter the cold walnut crumb topping across the fruit in an even blanket, pressing lightly.
8. **Bake to Thick Bubbles:** Bake on the lower oven rack for 60 to 75 minutes. The pie is done when the crumb topping is toasted and fragrant, the fruit feels tender when poked with a thin skewer, and the juices around the edges are bubbling thickly. If the walnuts begin browning too quickly after 40 minutes, drape a sheet of aluminum foil loosely over the top.
9. **Cool and Unmold:** Transfer the pan to a wire cooling rack and let cool completely. Run a thin knife around the inside rim, release the springform clasp, and remove the side ring before slicing.

## Notes

- **Serving Suggestion:** Serve slices at room temperature or slightly warmed, topped with a scoop of vanilla bean ice cream or a splash of chilled heavy pouring cream.
- **Storage:** Keeps covered loosely with a clean kitchen towel at room temperature for up to 3 days.
